<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later

  macOS codesign entitlements for Open-SSTV's PyInstaller bundle.

  Applied by ``.github/workflows/build.yml`` during the macOS build, to
  the ad-hoc-signed launcher (``dist/open-sstv/open-sstv``).  These
  entitlements are what tell AMFI (Apple Mobile File Integrity) that
  it's OK to load the ad-hoc-signed Python framework and ~200 Python
  extension ``.so`` files under ``_internal/`` from the same bundle.

  Without at minimum ``com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation``
  the launcher fails at first dlopen with::

      code signature in <...> '/path/to/_internal/Python' not valid for
      use in process: library load disallowed by system policy

  v0.6.14 audit S-L6: ``allow-dyld-environment-variables`` and
  ``allow-jit`` were dropped.  Combined with library-validation off and the
  microphone entitlement, DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES let any local process run
  code inside Open-SSTV and inherit its microphone (TCC) grant.  Nothing
  here needs either: CPython doesn't JIT, and nothing sets DYLD_* at launch.
  ``allow-unsigned-executable-memory`` stays — cffi (sounddevice) writes
  executable callback trampolines.

  This file is NOT a substitute for proper Developer ID signing +
  notarization — it just makes ad-hoc signing viable on modern macOS.
  See the "macOS codesigning" follow-up task for the notarized path.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Needed ONLY while the bundle is ad-hoc signed: each ad-hoc signature
      has its own identity, so library validation would reject every
      bundled .so/.dylib.  Remove once Developer ID signing exists (all
      code then shares one Team ID).
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- cffi (sounddevice callbacks) allocates executable trampolines. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <!--
      Audio input (RX).  Under the hardened runtime, macOS refuses to even
      prompt for microphone access without this entitlement — the TCC log
      says so outright:

          Prompting policy for hardened runtime; service:
          kTCCServiceMicrophone requires entitlement
          com.apple.security.device.audio-input but it is missing

      and the denial is silent from the app's side: the input stream opens
      fine and delivers nothing but zeroes.  Applies to every audio input,
      including virtual loopback devices like BlackHole (issue #35).
      Pairs with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in open_sstv.spec.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
